It's a CNCF-certified Kubernetes distribution developed by Rancher Labs and now part of Cloud Native Computing Foundation.

"What we're using is K3s, a lightweight Kubernetes distribution. 
In real companies you may see kubeadm clusters, managed Kubernetes like Amazon EKS, Azure Kubernetes Service, or Google Kubernetes Engine. 
But the Kubernetes objects and manifests remain the same."

It uses the same Kubernetes APIs.
=======================================================================
run hostname -i first and then put the ip in node ip in the below command.

curl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | \
INSTALL_K3S_EXEC="server \
--node-ip=172.20.10.3 \
--flannel-iface=enp0s3 \
--disable traefik \
--disable servicelb" \
sh -

check "which k3s" or "sudo systemctl status k3s"

sudo systemctl status k3s

kubectl get nodes -o wide
========================================================================
->kubectl get nodes

Expected:

NAME     STATUS
server   Ready

This is our Node.

Cluster Info:

kubectl cluster-info

System Pods:

kubectl get pods -A
=============================================================================
Create Pod directly.

kubectl run nginx --image=nginx

Check:

kubectl get pods

Output:

nginx   Running

Describe:

kubectl describe pod nginx

Show:

Image
IP
Events

Logs:

kubectl logs nginx

Delete:

kubectl delete pod nginx

Create Again:

kubectl run apache --image=httpd

Check:

kubectl get pods

Show Container Inside Pod
sudo kubectl get pod nginx -o wide

sudo kubectl logs nginx

Exec Inside Pod
sudo kubectl exec -it nginx -- bash
